<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>UTExportedTypeDeclarations</key>
  <array><dict>
    <key>UTTypeIdentifier</key><string>com.pktstorm.headstate.measurements-jsonl</string>
    <key>UTTypeDescription</key><string>Headstate measurement report</string>
    <key>UTTypeConformsTo</key><array><string>public.text</string></array>
    <key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key><dict>
      <key>public.filename-extension</key><array><string>jsonl</string></array>
      <key>public.mime-type</key><string>application/x-ndjson</string>
    </dict>
  </dict></array>
  <!-- Merged into the generated Xcode project's Info.plist by the Tauri
       CLI. LAN discovery of the paired desktop (#511): without this key
       iOS silently drops every multicast and every connection to a
       private address, so mDNS finds nothing and pairing "just fails". -->
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Headstate Companion looks for your desktop Headstate on the local network to pair with it and send it commands.</string>
  <!-- The Bonjour service the desktop advertises (remote/discovery.rs).
       iOS refuses to browse a service type not declared here. -->
  <key>NSBonjourServiceTypes</key>
  <array>
    <string>_headstate._tcp</string>
  </array>
  <!-- Opportunistic background refresh (plugins/headstate-refresh,
       #516). BGTaskScheduler refuses to register or submit a task whose
       identifier is not listed here, and grants app-refresh windows
       only to an app that declares the `fetch` background mode. This is
       a window a few seconds long every fifteen minutes at best, not a
       stream; nothing here keeps the app alive. -->
  <key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
  <array>
    <string>com.pktstorm.headstate.companion.refresh</string>
  </array>
  <key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
  <array>
    <string>fetch</string>
  </array>
  <!-- The step-up signing keys (plugins/headstate-keys) carry a
       `.userPresence` access control, so signing raises the system's
       Face ID / Touch ID / passcode sheet. Without this key iOS refuses
       Face ID for the app outright and every destructive command fails
       with an authentication error instead of a prompt. -->
  <key>NSFaceIDUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Headstate Companion confirms it is you before deleting anything on your desktop.</string>
  <!-- The pairing QR is scanned with the camera (tauri-plugin-barcode-
       scanner, #514). iOS terminates an app that opens the camera
       without this string. Wording from docs/mobile-store-listings.md. -->
  <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Headstate Companion uses the camera to scan the pairing code shown on your desktop.</string>
  <!-- Export compliance, answered here so an upload is not asked again.

       `false` because App Store Connect's App Encryption Documentation
       questionnaire was completed for this app and concluded that NO
       documentation needs to be uploaded, on the condition that the app
       is not available in France (see docs/mobile-release-process.md).

       `false` and `true` are not a judgement call here; they are a pair
       with the code. Apple issues an ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode
       only for documentation it has REVIEWED, and it refuses any upload
       that declares `true` without a matching code:

         Invalid Export Compliance Code. The export compliance key value
         [] in the app's Info.plist doesn't match the key value of the
         app's export compliance documentation.

       Builds 10, 11 and 12 all hit that, with the code key empty, then
       absent, then absent with the questionnaire answered. The constant
       was `true`. With no documentation on file there is no code, so
       `true` cannot be satisfied and `false` is the only answer that
       matches what App Store Connect actually holds.

       What the app does with cryptography has not changed: TLS 1.3 to
       the paired desktop over rustls, ECDSA P-256 and ML-DSA-65
       signatures authenticating commands, and Stronghold plus an
       AES-GCM key protecting the app's own secrets at rest. The
       questionnaire was answered against exactly that, and its verdict
       is what this key now reports.

       Revisit if the app gains a user-facing encryption feature or
       carries third-party data: re-run the questionnaire, and if it then
       requires documentation, `true` plus the code it issues.

       Separately, and not satisfied by anything in this repository: US
       exporters of 5D002 software owe BIS an annual self-classification
       report, due 1 February for the preceding calendar year. -->
  <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
